### 3.3 Total system and sequential-kernel transitions

For every valid system state and legal environment event:

```text
Step_E(Sigma, event)
  = NoAdvance(ordinary_observation)
  | Advance(Sigma', trace_delta, ordinary_observation)

Inv(Sigma) and event in Legal_E(Sigma)
  implies Inv(Sigma') or NoAdvance
```

`ProtocolStep_E` covers bounded handoff, effect claim/delivery/settlement,
template publication, persistence/restart, cancellation and shutdown. Its
trace may observe a previously committed effect or coherent read receipt, but
it cannot change whether the corresponding authority transition happened.
An external request can span several system steps; its linearization point is
the named authority Commit/read cut or an ordinary pre-authority disposition.
When `U = NoAuthority`, no kernel command is legal: protocol steps may enqueue
within the fixed startup bound, observe readiness, cancel, or close requests.
`Assemble_E` is the sole transition that constructs `LiveAuthority(Omega)`.
During `Initializing`, only replay, reliable ordered chain control and the
capability/effect work they create may invoke the kernel; ordinary controller
handlers remain queued. `Ready_E` exposes the dispatcher only after replay.
Draining first stops new checkout/ingress, then consumes `Omega` only after
capability and effect closure. Startup failure never fabricates an empty live
authority merely to answer queued callers.

For every valid authority/capability state and legal kernel command:

```text
KernelStep_E(Omega, command)
  = NoAuthorityCommit((A, K'), ordinary_disposition)
  | AuthorityCommit((A', K'), committed_effects, ordinary_disposition)

Omega in InvKernel and command in LegalKernel
  implies the returned Omega' is in InvKernel
```

`NoAuthorityCommit` means exactly that `A` is unchanged. `K` may still change
because a stale/cancelled capability is consumed or an owner-free request
returns its compute permit. Treating that discharge as a completely unchanged
state would leak linear capabilities in the reference model.

Legal input has no `IntegrityStop` branch. An integrity fault denotes a broken
trusted premise, invalid pre-state or programmer defect with a mechanical
unreachability proof. Stale evidence, policy rejection, resource/capacity
pressure, allocation failure, cancellation and external failure are closed
ordinary outcomes owned by the producing operation.

The model does not hide those outcomes in one generic error. Each command
family closes over the following operation-owned algebra; M1 gives every row a
concrete Rust enum and proves its consumers exhaustive:

| Operation | Ordinary dispositions that must be representable | Outside `Legal` only |
| --- | --- | --- |
| startup/assembly | running with one complete authority, authority-configuration/startup rejection with no authority, optional derived component disabled/degraded, missing/corrupt/unreadable persistence fallback, cancellation before readiness and closure of pre-start requests | two live authority generations, partial authority-task publication or forged consensus/static configuration evidence |
| controller handoff | enqueued, queue full, channel closed, cancelled before handling, response delivered or responder abandoned after the selected disposition | one request simultaneously queued and handler-owned, or an abandoned response changing whether a kernel commit occurred |
| retained Remote/Proposal ingress | retained, exact duplicate/promotion, policy reject, bounded resource exclusion, acknowledged handoff release, cancelled before ownership | forged sealed admission evidence or invalid authority pre-state |
| Local/TestAccept | accepted/read-only success, duplicate, policy/verification reject, bounded resource exclusion, relevant-evidence stale, cancellation | impossible source/result pairing or forged validation receipt |
| compute checkout/settlement | no eligible work, checked out, continued Resolve-to-Verify, Ready, Waiting(Missing), ordinary reject, relevant stale discharge, bounded cancellation/resource outcome | duplicate mutation right or malformed sealed receipt |
| final membership/RBF/capacity | accepted, rejected with exact reason/effect, reresolve, relevant stale, bounded exclusion before mutation | partial replacement, charge mismatch or invalid projection pre-state |
| administration/ban/expiry | applied cohort, absent/no-op, bounded cancellation/resource outcome; every Remote death settles relay state | cause/owner pair unconstructible by sealed types |
| chain control | exact reconciliation/clear, deterministic recovery exclusion/fallback, relevant stale preparation, cancellation before a committed cut | consensus-invalid ordered command or impossible projection contradiction |
| coherent read | value, absence/fallback, optional derived field unavailable, allocation/resource error local to the request | incoherent authority pre-state; a derived arithmetic failure is never generation invalidity |
| effect protocol | claimed, delivered, endpoint-disposed, acknowledged/settled, capacity-level wait, closed after ordered drain | duplicate claim or producer close while an undisposed sole capability exists |
| relay/template/persistence protocols | acknowledged/released/rebuilt, published/stale, saved/replayed/rejected/IO-failed, cancellation with exact residual | boundary state that violates its sealed ownership or trusted consensus premise |
| shutdown/restart | clean durable release or explicit persistence prohibition after proven sole-capability/integrity loss | silently persisting an unaccounted authority state |

An allocator error means a fallible bounded construction returned an error;
process-wide allocator abort remains in the explicit process-integrity
residual. `Await(level)` is an owned protocol state with a named releaser, not
an error-loop disposition. A step may return `Stale`, but an external request
cannot retry forever against the same semantic read set.

`Plan` may perform fallible scratch allocation or reserve physical container
capacity, but it does not change `ObsKernel(Omega)`. A `PreparedDelta` retains the
exclusive authority borrow and is consumed once by total infallible Apply.
Lock-external evidence is revalidated while constructing the plan. The model
therefore does not invent an Apply-time OCC failure that production cannot
have.

### 3.4 Safety and conservation kernel

Let `O` be the owner map and `C.rows` the per-owner charge rows:

```text
Dom(O) = Dom(C.rows)
for every h in Dom(O): C.rows[h] = charge_record(O[h])
C.aggregates = CheckedHierarchicalFold(C.rows)
effect_usage = CheckedFold(committed_effect_log)
```

`CheckedHierarchicalFold` covers total preacceptance, Remote, per-peer,
replacement-history, Accepted and active-work partitions. It is a partial
commutative algebra: checked overflow or a configured limit yields an ordinary
pre-Apply outcome. The effect log has its own checked partition algebra and is
not charged through a transaction-owner row.

Every numeric dimension is distinct in the model: `PayloadBytes`,
`ResolvedResidentBytes`, `WeightedResidentBytes`, `EffectBytes`,
`EncodedEdges`, `ExpandedEdges`, `Cycles`, `Entries` and `ActiveWork`. A
conversion is a named checked function owned by the residency/effect policy.
The current payload-only compute grant versus metadata-inclusive retained
charge finding is exactly the class of bug that an untyped `usize` equation
would hide.

Every materialized index, membership relation, dependency frontier, scheduler
ticket/cursor and source version has an explicit transition invariant. A
derived structure may be incrementally maintained, but never decides owner
truth. Its model must define either reconstruction from authoritative facts or
an exact replay law for history-dependent state.

Monotonic identifiers also keep separate meanings: one `ApplyStamp` per
atomic committed delta, one non-reused `EntryVersion` per owner incarnation,
one `Arrival` per first admission, one `ChainRevision` per installed view and
one `PoolGeneration` per generation replacement. `DependencyCut` is derived
from a committed Apply stamp. Batch members cannot consume fictitious Apply
stamps merely to obtain unique owner versions. Finite production counters need
one checked exhaustion theorem or ordinary rollover boundary; mathematical
natural numbers may not silently erase that refinement obligation.

At stable cuts, capability conservation is:

```text
O[h] = PreAccepted(Computing(v))  implies |Caps(h, v)| = 1
for every (h, v): |Caps(h, v)| <= 1
Stale(c, A) implies MutationRight(c, A) = empty

FreeComputePermits + |HeldComputePermits| = ConfiguredComputePermits
RetainedComputingOwners = |CurrentRetainedWorkCapabilities(A)|
                       = C.aggregates.active_work
```

The reverse implication is intentionally absent. A chain/admin transition may
retire an owner while its unique worker capability remains live. That stale
capability has no mutation right and must retire in bounded work. Checkout
creates the Computing state and releases its capability atomically at the
stable cut. Effect publication, ordered chain command ownership and any exact
pre-authority handoff require analogous single-claim laws.

Owner-free Local/TestAccept computation holds one bounded compute permit and
one request capability but creates no transaction owner or charge row. The
sole effect publisher has at most one global claim; an endpoint lease names
the exact current log token while the committed record remains in `A` until a
settlement kernel step advances or removes it. Every accepted ordered chain
command and pre-authority relay handoff is in exactly one queued, claimed,
committed/released or terminal location. These are separate conservation laws,
not instances of the transaction-owner partition.

Every controller command payload obeys one ownership partition. API enqueue
acknowledgement is a separate observation because no-responder notifications
and `Reconcile` may return successfully while their payload remains queued:

```text
for every command payload p:
  |CallerOwned(p) + Queued(p) + HandlerOwned(p) + Terminal(p)| = 1

EnqueueObservation(p)
  in {NotAttempted, Accepted, Full, Closed, CallerCancelled}

for each optional responder s attached to p:
  ResponderState(s) in {Attached(p), Sent, Dropped}
  ReceiverState(s) in {Waiting, Observed, Abandoned}

|QueuedPayloads| <= ControllerQueueBound
|HandlerOwnedPayloads| <= DispatcherConcurrencyBound
|QueuedChainCommands| <= OrderedChainQueueBound
```

Payload bytes and collection cardinality remain separate cost terms; a fixed
request count is not by itself a peer-memory proof. A dropped response endpoint
changes only the protocol delivery trace. It cannot undo, suppress or repeat a
kernel commit. Remote/Proposal known and in-flight ownership has the stronger
cross-crate partition already stated above and must be released on a pre-
authority full/closed/cancel outcome.

A bounded carrier is not an atomic authority command by implication. The
current Proposal notification batch becomes an ordered sequence of per-item
admission attempts only after the handler owns it. Every item keeps its own
relay known/in-flight obligation; handler cancellation or a terminal fault
after a committed prefix must release or settle the untouched suffix rather
than treating the enqueue acknowledgement as an all-items commit. M1 models
this small handoff automaton explicitly; `D1-KNOWN-HANDOFF` is its current
counterexample.

M0 also records the current request-cost boundary rather than treating a
bounded channel as a proof of bounded payload work:

| Request family | Production bound before the tx-pool queue | Remaining model obligation |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Proposal notification batch | `NotifyTxBatch` checks both `MAX_RELAY_TXS_NUM_PER_BATCH` and `MAX_RELAY_TXS_BYTES_PER_BATCH` before enqueue | preserve the validated carrier and exact known/in-flight settlement |
| Remote relay transaction | the relay frame is capped at 4 MiB and requested-hash state is bounded; one transaction is submitted per async request | bind the single transaction to the tx-pool transaction-size disposition before it can occupy a queued/handler residency budget |
| Local/TestAccept/IntegrationTest transaction | RPC request bytes are bounded by configured `max_request_body_size`, but the public controller accepts an arbitrary already-built `TransactionView` and non-contextual size rejection occurs only in the handler | define whether the in-process caller is trusted for payload residency; every network/RPC route still needs a pre-queue byte bound, and source compatibility constrains the carrier design |
| Fresh/compact/fetch proposal IDs | ordinary compact-block and peer-request paths have consensus/protocol count bounds | the public `Vec`/`HashSet` carrier has no bound, and the 100 ms relayer prune can aggregate many individually bounded peer requests; model the aggregate count/byte bound instead of borrowing one-message limits |
| Ordered chain reconciliation | capacity-one reliable lane carries the complete detached/attached fork and paired snapshot | count is one but retained bytes and preparation work are proportional to actual fork depth and block payload; trusted consensus origin does not imply a constant-size command |
| Full-pool/detail/fee queries | request payload is constant-size; accepted ownership bounds the response population | bound simultaneous handler/output residency and authority read-lock work separately; RPC batch/connection policy is an external premise only when explicitly configured |

The ordinary queue bound is 512 and current handler concurrency is
`2 * max_tx_verify_workers` after checked construction. These count bounds do
not cap an arbitrary Rust API payload, a fork command, or an O(pool) response.
M1 models their protocol locations; M2 must publish count, byte, lock-hold and
amplification equations for every row.

Evidence validity is monotonic only inside its named context. Raw evidence may
be enriched to resolved, verified and membership evidence; a relevant chain,
rules, owner-version, dependency-source or policy cut invalidates exactly the
affected proof. A global cut that invalidates semantically unrelated work is a
performance defect; a missing cut is a correctness defect.

Formally, a receipt owns its semantic read set rather than an implementation
version shortcut:

```text
r = (subject_identity, evidence_context, read_set, observed_values)
Current_A(r) iff Project(A, r.read_set) = r.observed_values
```

A version/source cut is an implementation of `Current_A`, not its definition.
It may conservatively create false staleness only if M2 proves bounded progress
and acceptable cost; it may never accept a changed relevant fact. The current
global Accepted cut on owner-free negative direct validation is therefore a
refinement/cost finding, while an omitted dependency loss is a safety finding.

### 3.5 Dynamic footprint and composition theorem

The semantic footprint of command `x` at authority `A` is not only `W/P/R/H`:

```text
Footprint_Omega(x) = (
  raw_and_witness_identity,
  consumed_and_produced_cells,
  expanded_cell_and_header_reads,
  pool_parent_reader_spender_edges,
  proposal_short_id,
  chain_rules_dependency_time_since_dao_and_maturity_cuts,
  fee_cycles_size_and_package_order_metrics,
  source_peer_deadline_and_fairness_policy,
  global_peer_and_effect_resource_vectors,
  accepted_capacity_and_eviction_frontier,
  externally_committed_effect_class)
```

Independence is a transient proof at one authority cut, never a stored Boolean
or a second DAG. A cohort receives exactly one class:

1. `IndependentComposable`: logical member transitions commute and retain the
   same per-member dispositions; a canonical compiler assigns exact versions,
   arrivals and effects.
2. `CanonicalOrdered`: order is observable, but one read-only Plan simulates
   the complete named sequential fold before one Apply.
3. `Coupled`: RBF, dependency, eviction, chain or indivisible-effect semantics
   require one joint component plan.

For every accepted compiled cohort `X`:

```text
ObsKernel(CommitBatch_E(Omega, X))
  = ObsKernel(FoldNoInterleave(KernelStep_E, Omega, Canon_Omega(X)))
```

The same initial `Omega` and absence of an intervening kernel command are
load-bearing premises. Fold intermediates are hidden by the atomic batch. This
is one legal linearization, not equality with every concurrent interleaving.
Failure to construct a batch proof is an ordinary mutation-free fallback to
the exact coupled planner.

For two candidates, logical commutativity is the stronger sufficient test:

```text
T_x(Omega) = the post-state, per-command disposition and logical effects
             returned by KernelStep_E(Omega, x)

Commutes_Omega(x, y) iff
  CoreObs(T_y(T_x(Omega))) = CoreObs(T_x(T_y(Omega)))
  and both orders return the same disposition for x and y
```

`CoreObs` includes logical effects but not their serialized sequence number;
the batch compiler then emits those effects in `Canon_Omega` order. This avoids the
false conclusion that two disjoint admissions never commute merely because
their public callbacks require a deterministic order. Conversely, a common
final state without equal per-member dispositions is not commutativity.

### 3.6 Liveness, fairness, cancellation and crash boundary

There is no global decreasing rank for an indefinitely running service.
Instead, under explicitly named executor fairness and environment premises,
each request, command or linear capability has a local finite rank while its
evidence epoch is stable, or waits on a monotonic level with one named
transition that can release it. A new view may create a new obligation, but
the superseded obligation retires in bounded work.

Retry against the same authority cut, evidence epoch and source level is
forbidden. An `Instant`-based deadline may use process-monotonic advance as its
level. The current Remote/Accepted expiry and `accepted_at` ordering use Unix
wall time instead, so their progress theorem is conditional on the environment
eventually observing a value at least as large as the recorded deadline; a
clock rollback is not a level advance. Effect wait uses committed publication/
settlement progress; scheduler wait uses the authoritative work level.
Allocator availability has no monotonic source or unique releaser, so fixed-
delay reconstruction is not a liveness proof.

Fairness is stated per source and capability class. Strict Ready policy, Remote
expiry and Local compute-gate service cannot be summarized as one universal
no-starvation claim. Shutdown adds a separate finite protocol: stop producers,
settle or invalidate every move-only capability, close and drain effects, then
release persistence. Crash durability remains outside in-process atomicity and
must match the explicit residual-risk and restart projection.

The temporal claims use these shapes rather than prose-only "eventually":

```text
Init_E(Sigma) implies Inv(Sigma)
Inv(Sigma) and Sigma --LegalStep--> Sigma' implies Inv(Sigma')

StableEvidenceEpoch(o) and FairlyScheduled(o)
  implies eventually(Done(o) or NamedWait(o) or OrdinaryTerminal(o))

StableTemplateSources and FairTemplateDriver
  implies eventually(PublishedSources = CurrentSources)

CommittedEffect(e) and FairPublisherCircuit
  implies eventually(Published(e) or CircuitDisposed(e))
```

`NamedWait` records both a source level and the transition family that can
advance it. Notification is only a lossy hint: the consumer subscribes before
reading the level and sleeps only if the level is unchanged. Crash has its own
explicit transition `Restart_E(PersistProjection(A), DurableProjection(D))`;
volatile committed effects may be lost or an in-flight endpoint repeated only within the documented
residual boundary.

The endpoint circuit model also counts foreign work after timeout. Each
one-way circuit may leave at most one detached blocking operation for its
endpoint; opening the circuit forbids later operations from accumulating more.
That detached operation has no owner/capability/authority guard and cannot
veto Apply, but remains part of the shutdown/resource cost boundary.

### 3.7 Quantitative and adversarial model

For `B` owners, `E` dependency/conflict edges and exact coupled components
`G`, every candidate implementation publishes a static upper bound:

```text
Cost(B, E, G)
  <= C_fixed + B * C_item + E * C_edge
     + sum(C_coupled(size(g)) for g in G)
```

Separate equations cover authority lock hold, allocation, retained bytes,
active work, wakeups, retries, stale/wasted compute and external-effect
backlog. Hostile inputs control graph shape, peer partition and ordering, so
the proof includes deep chains, wide fanout, large RBF/eviction components,
short-id collisions, full partitions and repeated view changes.

The static bound is not fitted to benchmark data. Topology selection may also
consume measured workload parameters such as completion inter-arrival gaps,
simultaneously available completions, Ready cohort occupancy, verification
duration distribution, worker utilization and idle time. Those measurements
choose among implementations that already satisfy the bound; they cannot
weaken it. Existing Ready-attempt/work counts are only a cohort-occupancy
proxy and do not establish completion clustering or verification-duration
distribution. M2 therefore defines the missing measurement schema and M4 may
add feature-gated counters before the final profiling phase.

Two security hyperproperties supplement single-trace invariants:

- disjoint traffic cannot change another peer's disposition except through a
  named shared resource or fairness policy;
- one admitted hostile item cannot cause uncharged or unbounded repeated work;
  repeated validation requires a strictly newer evidence cut or consumes a
  finite retry/cancellation budget.

### 3.8 Mechanical refinement and model-delta review

The proof chain is directional:

```text
CKB semantics and compatibility
  -> mathematical transition system
  -> independently written pure Rust reference model
  -> production refinement mapping
  -> focused property/concurrency falsifiers
  -> complete boundary and integration regressions
  -> static cost counters
  -> profiling and fixed-binary A/B
```

The reference model must not call production transition code. Generated gates
bind model variants, production constructors/consumers and exact evidence so a
second hand-maintained specification cannot silently drift. Mutation or
deliberate-fault checks must demonstrate that key properties fail when their
premise is removed.

Before M2 closes, the model receives an adversarial review independent of the
transition author's happy-path examples. At minimum a separately written trace
generator must search legal hostile schedules, remove each root premise in
turn and produce a shortest counterexample. A reviewer or agent who did not
author the transition algebra must also inspect the invariant basis before M4
design freeze. Existing production tests may seed traces but cannot define the
expected model result.

Every phase boundary performs a model-delta audit before reviewing the latest
code diff:

1. Did a counterexample expose an omitted legal input, observation or trusted
   premise?
2. Can any state, invariant or protocol be derived from a smaller basis and
   removed?
3. Was the model weakened merely to accept the current implementation?
4. Does the production refinement cover every model transition and reject
   every illegal one?
5. Did a new field, state, task, queue, lock, version or effect add a theorem
   and a measured cost owner?
6. Do liveness and adversarial bounds still hold under the complete system,
   not only the newest slice?

A bad trace accepted by model and production is a model gap. A trace rejected
by the model but accepted by production is a refinement bug. A violated
trusted premise is a boundary-contract bug. Changing the model reopens every
dependent proof; it never creates a local exception in production.

### 3.9 Model quality gate

The model itself passes review only when all of these are true:

- **Sound abstraction:** every abstract field has an extraction function from
  CKB/production types, and omitting it has either an impossibility proof or a
  counterexample. VM internals are abstracted only behind exact evidence.
- **Semantic completeness:** every historical bug, suppressed report, public
  behavior and current section 16 finding is expressible as a state or trace;
  an unrepresentable incident proves the model incomplete.
- **Small invariant basis:** each invariant is independent or mechanically
  derivable from named earlier laws. Redundant flags, mirrored owner state and
  duplicate clocks are rejected.
- **Counterexample value:** bounded exploration produces the shortest
  transaction/peer/view trace and identifies whether the fault is model,
  refinement or boundary. A property that can never be falsified is not useful.
- **Compositional scale:** finite small-scope exploration handles identities
  and interleavings; algebraic/inductive proofs carry resource, graph and batch
  bounds to configured limits. Passing only tiny examples is insufficient.
- **Independent implementation:** the reference model shares public semantic
  vocabulary and generated bindings, but no production transition/planner
  implementation.
- **Quantitative relevance:** every retained state/protocol contributes a term
  to safety, liveness or cost. A model artifact with no theorem or decision
  consequence is removed.
- **Maintainable drift control:** generated discovery detects a new production
  constructor, state, command, public observation or effect that has no model
  mapping, and detects a model term with no semantic/attack owner.

Both columns are hard gates. Neither substitutes for the other.

### Safety and compatibility

- `TxPoolAuthority` remains the sole transaction lifecycle owner.
- Ownership, exact charge, projections and committed effects change in one
  total Apply; no undo or partially committed replacement exists.
- RBF, dependency, capacity, chain-view and effect rules retain their exact
  current behavior unless a separately reviewed compatibility decision says
  otherwise.
- Validation and external I/O remain outside the authority guard.
- Legal or hostile input, staleness, allocation/resource pressure and external
  failure remain typed local outcomes and cannot stop the service.
- No second lifecycle DAG, shard owner, mutable decision cache, unbounded
  queue, lock across await or panic-and-catch protocol is introduced.

### Semantic execution objective

- Transaction read/write facts constructively distinguish work that can run
  independently from work that requires an exact coupled cohort.
- For a bounded set of chain-backed, pairwise-commuting transactions,
  authority transition work scales with committed batches rather than one
  complete authority sequence per transaction.
- Independent work can occupy all configured compute workers without a
  per-transaction serial authority round trip between otherwise parallel
  stages.
- RBF, pool-produced dependencies, conditional reader/spender relationships,
  capacity eviction, stale views and effect pressure fall back to the same
  coupled authority algebra without duplicated policy.
- The independent path is a specialization of the one Plan/Apply kernel, not a
  second feature model.
- Single-item latency requires no artificial timer. Batching is opportunistic
  over an already non-empty bounded level or an existing bounded ingress batch.

No phase may be called architecture-complete until both groups pass.

### Whole-system concurrency law

Concurrency is a property of the complete tx-pool lifecycle, not a Ready-lane
optimization. Every stage from ingress through query/template publication must
derive its serialization boundary from the same CKB transaction semantics:

- immutable/non-authoritative work runs concurrently whenever its exact chain
  view, rules and bounded resource capability permit it;
- authoritative work for disjoint transaction footprints is compiled as a
  bounded ordered or commuting batch and pays one coherent Apply cut;
- RBF, pool-produced dependency cohorts, shared inputs, conditional
  reader/spender relations, capacity eviction and indivisible effects are the
  named reasons for coupled serialization;
- a global version, queue round trip, actor, lock or implementation phase is
  not by itself evidence that two transactions conflict;
- admission, checkout, settlement, membership, dependency wake, effect
  publication, query projection and template update must each preserve this
  distinction. A false conflict at any one stage defeats end-to-end
  concurrency even when verification itself is parallel;
- chain full/reset publication retains its required serialization, while
  optimistic uncle/proposal/transaction partial template lanes remain
  concurrent and may discard stale work. The architecture must not serialize
  those lanes merely to simplify authority reasoning;
- Local/TestAccept remains owner-free during compute and participates in the
  same fair bounded compute gate. Retained batch throughput may not starve it;
- no separate DAG, shard owner or fast-path authority is allowed. Ephemeral
  footprint proofs specialize one authority and disappear after Plan.

D1-D3 must therefore audit every serial cut, not only Ready Apply. Each cut
must map to a named semantic conflict or compatibility rule and one falsifier.
A cut with no such owner is accidental serialization and blocks design freeze.

### Layer necessity precedes concurrency

The current module/stage topology is evidence to audit, not an architecture to
preserve by default. Before asking how work inside a layer can run concurrently,
D1/D2 must prove that the layer itself is necessary.

Start from irreducible semantic obligations rather than current type names:

1. accept or reject an external handoff while bounding hostile residency;
2. resolve exact chain/pool dependency evidence under one view;
3. execute script verification under exact rules and limits;
4. decide membership, RBF, dependency and budget changes atomically;
5. publish only effects produced by a committed transition;
6. reconcile ordered chain changes and derive query/template views.

These are obligations, not a claim that six runtime layers are required. Every
current stage, lifecycle state, task, queue, lock, journal and version must show
one distinct semantic owner: validation evidence, move-only capability,
compatibility boundary, unavoidable failure domain or concurrency boundary.
If two adjacent stages carry no distinct authoritative decision, fuse them. If
a state exists only to transport data that can remain in one typed capability,
remove it. If a task or queue has no independent progress owner, reuse the
existing bounded executor/level. If an obligation can be derived as a read-only
projection, it cannot become mutable authority.

The D2 comparison must therefore contain both a layer-deletion table and a
serial-cut table. D3 may freeze a concurrency model only after every surviving
layer is proven necessary and every removed layer has compatibility evidence.
Passing tests for the current topology is not a necessity proof.

The earlier minimality audit remains evidence, but only for semantic lower
bounds. It proved that hostile ingress must be owned and charged before work,
that exact resolution and script-verification evidence cannot be omitted,
that compute release must overlap later membership work, and that external I/O
may consume only committed effects. It did not prove that `ingress`, `resolve`,
`verify`, `Ready` and `effect` must each remain a distinct resident state,
queue, task or Apply boundary. In particular, the measured direct-finalization
failure held the compute permit through membership and therefore cannot
falsify a finished-work capability that releases the permit before bounded
batch settlement. A mechanism being useful inside the inherited topology is
not proof that the topology boundary is necessary. D2 must preserve these
semantic lower bounds while independently attempting every safe fusion or
deletion; otherwise the same review error is repeated.

### Five root proof laws

The observations above are not separate mechanisms or phases. D1-D3 reduce
them to five root laws:

1. **Exact Evidence.** Every decision consumes immutable evidence bound to the
   exact transaction, chain view, script rules and observed read set. Evidence
   grows monotonically from raw to resolved to verified to membership facts;
   only a relevant source change invalidates it. CKB inputs, dep-group
   expansion, headers, `since`/time context and pool-produced cells live here.
2. **Atomic Authority.** One transaction has one authoritative location.
   Ownership, charge, membership/dependency projections and committed effects
   change together in one total Apply. Projections cannot decide ownership;
   ordinary rejection, stale work, pressure and allocation cannot become a
   structural generation fault.
3. **Bounded Composition.** Every cohort relation is typed as
   `IndependentComposable`, `CanonicalOrdered` or `Coupled`. An independently
   composable cohort has order-invariant logical dispositions and results;
   tokens and public effects are still emitted in canonical order. A
   canonically ordered cohort may depend on policy, capacity, eviction or
   effect placement but one read-only Plan simulates the exact canonical
   sequential result; RBF, pool-produced dependencies, shared inputs,
   closures and indivisible effects use the coupled compiler. Aggregate and
   per-owner resource algebra is part of this proof.
4. **Owned Progress.** Every resident state, task, queue, lock, wait and Apply
   boundary has one necessary progress owner and one named releaser. Otherwise
   it fuses or disappears. This law covers Local fairness, stragglers, stale
   cuts, effect pressure, chain control, cancellation, shutdown and bounded
   wasted work without wait-to-fill timers or repair loops.
5. **Mechanical Evidence.** A sequential reference model, property/concurrency
   tests, generated producer/consumer gates, complete compatibility regressions
   and static cost equations prove the first four laws. Human-maintained lists
   and benchmark results cannot substitute for a derivable proof.

Standing rule: whenever a constraint can be expressed as state, sets,
functions, conservation, equivalence, partial order or well-founded descent,
write it first as an executable mathematical model and mechanical check.
Prose may explain assumptions, boundaries and rationale, but cannot be its sole
correctness evidence.

Semantic, topology and commit minimality remain three evaluation axes, not
three additional rule sets. No proof on one axis closes another. Relay, RPC,
persistence, proposal/template, verification-cache and consensus-validation
compatibility are boundary obligations checked against all five laws.

Disjoint cell footprints alone do not prove `IndependentComposable`: accepted
capacity, source priority, eviction and effect-region pressure can still make
order observable. Conversely, order dependence does not require one Apply per
member when Plan computes the complete canonical serialization before
mutation. The architecture target is therefore a bounded
commuting-or-canonically-ordered Plan/Apply kernel.

### Anti-bypass gate

The five laws are normative gates, not review prose. Each law must have a
stable identifier, one semantic implementation owner, at least one falsifier
and a generated evidence check. Phase progress is blocked as follows:

- M0 must close trusted domains, legal inputs, stable observations and exact
  compatibility boundaries before the reference transition is accepted;
- M1 must close the sequential model, invariant basis, resource/effect algebra
  and closed ordinary/fault boundary before composition is accepted;
- M2 must close capability/progress, dynamic composition and adversarial cost
  theorems before any topology is compared;
- M3 must close the obligation/layer, wait/releaser, capability-handoff,
  layer-deletion, `develop` refinement and static cost tables before one model
  can win;
- M4 must freeze proof types, generated evidence and implementation slices
  before production code changes;
- every implementation phase must derive from its Git diff all added states,
  tasks, queues, locks, versions, effects and panic-like operations. An added
  item with no root-law owner blocks the phase;
- operation-count gates must fail when bounded ingress, compute exchange or
  membership silently returns to one complete authority round trip per owner.

Compilation and existing test success cannot advance a phase without these
artifacts. Progress documents record the evidence checkpoint, not a subjective
percentage or a weaker milestone such as safety cutover.

## 4. Transaction-semantic derivation

M0-M2 must derive, rather than assume, the following immutable facts:

- consumed input outpoints: exclusive writes and RBF conflict keys;
- produced output outpoints: possible causal parents;
- direct cell deps and expanded dep-group cells: read dependencies;
- header deps: chain-view reads;
- pool-produced inputs/deps: causal or conditional pool relationships;
- exact `ChainViewId`, script-rule generation and time/DAO context;
- source/trust/peer policy and deterministic economic order;
- count/byte/edge/cycle/active-work/per-peer resource footprint;
- exact committed effect footprint.

Independence is not one stored boolean. The design must use ephemeral typed
proofs with distinct meanings:

1. `ComputeParallel`: immutable resolution or verification can execute beside
   other work under the same exact view and resource grants.
2. `MembershipCommuting`: candidate write sets are disjoint; no candidate is a
   causal/conditional parent, child, reader, spender or RBF victim of another;
   the complete batch fits without eviction; source order is a contiguous
   admissible prefix; and the combined effect fits.
3. `BatchCurrent`: every owner/version/phase/view receipt is still exact at
   the final authority cut.

Failure to construct any proof is an ordinary mutation-free fallback to the
coupled planner. Proofs are not retained lifecycle state.

The first semantic audit must also complete backward reachability from every
`AuthorityFault` constructor and every other production path that can stop an
authority generation or forbid persistence: invalid chain evidence, effect
lifecycle closure, worker/chain/publisher task exit or join failure, effect
close/drain failure and shutdown timeout. An optimization cannot hide an
input-reachable fault behind a faster path, and auditing only the inner fault
enum cannot prove service availability.

### 4.1 Canonical CKB Cell footprint

For transaction `t`, the model uses these exact immutable sets:

```text
W(t) = consumed input OutPoints
P(t) = output OutPoints derived from raw transaction hash and output index
R(t) = direct CellDep OutPoints plus every resolved dep-group member
H(t) = header dependency hashes
```

`W` is known from the raw transaction. Direct CellDeps are known at ingress,
but complete `R` is known only after dep-group resolution. `P` is known once
raw identity is fixed. `H` is a chain-view read. Witnesses affect script
verification and the verification-cache identity but do not change these Cell
sets.

For two distinct transactions `a` and `b`:

| Relation | CKB meaning | Compute | Final membership |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `W(a) intersect W(b)` | double spend / RBF conflict | may resolve and verify in parallel | coupled, canonical RBF policy |
| `W(a) intersect R(b)` or reverse | one transaction spends a Cell read by the other | may compute in parallel | conditional ordering edge; not a commuting admission |
| `P(a)` referenced by `W(b)` | unconfirmed causal parent | child resolution requires Accepted overlay | coupled parent/child graph |
| `P(a)` referenced by `R(b)` | unconfirmed CellDep producer | child resolution requires Accepted overlay | coupled dependency-reader graph |
| `R(a) intersect R(b)` | shared read-only Cell dependency | fully parallel | commuting if all other predicates hold |
| `H(a) intersect H(b)` | shared immutable header read | fully parallel under the exact view | commuting if both receipts remain current |
| disjoint Cell sets but aggregate pool/effect limit fails | global resource coupling | compute remains bounded and parallel | exact capacity/effect fallback |

`ExpandedFootprint` already canonicalizes `W`, expanded `R` and `H`, rejects
duplicate inputs, removes a dependency that is also the transaction's own
input, and enforces the edge grant. `ResolvedPayload` seals that footprint
with the exact resolved transaction. `VerifiedFacts` then binds it to the
dependency cut, chain view, script rules, time context and exact metrics. The
new execution model consumes these types; it does not reconstruct a nearby
footprint or cache a Boolean classification.

### 4.2 Why compute and membership have different independence rules

Resolve and Verify read immutable transaction/snapshot data and produce typed
evidence. Two different owner capabilities can compute concurrently even when
their transactions later share an input: neither worker mutates membership,
selects an RBF winner or releases a victim. Serializing such work would spend
the cost of a conflict before the authority has to decide it.

Final membership is different. It writes the accepted spender graph,
dependency readers, causal graph, global resource ledger and committed effect
log. It can batch only after the full `W/P/R/H` relation and aggregate limits
prove the changes commute. This establishes the separation between parallel
evidence production and atomic membership; it does not preselect a compute
coordinator, resident DAG or any other runtime topology.

## 5. Current-topology audit

For Remote, Proposal, Recovery and Local, trace every normal and hostile path
through:

`ingress -> retained charge -> resolve checkout -> resolve completion ->
verify checkout -> verify completion -> Ready -> membership -> effect
publication`.

For every edge record:

- semantic fact changed;
- owner and version before/after;
- authority read/write acquisitions;
- allocation, clone and projection work under the guard;
- permit held and when it is released;
- wake/task/channel boundary;
- whether another independent transaction can commute across it;
- which F1-F8 failure family requires the boundary;
- exact fallback and cancellation behavior.

An edge with no business, compatibility, attack or measured concurrency owner
is accidental ceremony. A boundary required per item but not per batch must be
represented as a batch delta rather than silently accepted as irreducible.

The audit compares current UAK, `develop`, the frozen pre-performance UAK and
the historical high-throughput pipeline checkpoint. Historical speed is not a
correctness argument, but it can reveal useful execution topology that the
safety cutover accidentally removed.

