## 6. Semantic transition algebra

Section 3 is the sole mathematical authority. This section specializes its
composition theorem to the current execution candidates; it must not restate
or weaken the model.

Retained ingress and compute checkout are provisionally
`CanonicalOrdered`. Common compute-completion transitions over distinct owners
may be `IndependentComposable`, but composition with new checkout remains
ordered because scheduler cursors and available slots are observable. Final
chain-backed membership is `IndependentComposable` only when its complete
dynamic footprint proof succeeds. RBF, capacity eviction, pool-produced
dependencies and chain transitions remain exact `Coupled` cohorts.

These are hypotheses to falsify, not stored classifications or permission to
implement. M0-M4 must prove the exact footprint predicate and the section 3.5
no-interleaving theorem before any candidate receives the name.

For every compiled batch, one Apply sequence identifies the atomic authority
transition. Each owner whose capability identity changes receives a distinct
non-reused `EntryVersion`; new owners receive distinct `Arrival` values in the
canonical batch order. Sequence is per Apply, version is per owner
incarnation, and arrival is per first admission. Those meanings must not be
encoded by one overloaded counter.

### 6.1 Stage-specific proof boundary

| Stage | Proof required | What may share one Apply | What forces exact fallback |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Retained ingress | bounded homogeneous source batch plus canonical item order | unique/duplicate/promoted/rejected Remote or Proposal outcomes simulated in that order | malformed Remote revokes that peer before any batch owner can survive; allocation/effect pressure is typed and mutation-free |
| Compute checkout | idle role capabilities, scheduler cut and virtual active-work budget | distinct queued owners assigned to compatible workers | no compatible worker, exhausted source/peer/global compute budget, stale dependency proof |
| Compute completion | exact move-only settlement token and current owner/version | distinct no-effect owner transitions, including Ready, requeue and direct Resolve-to-Verify handoff | peer-cohort revocation, terminal publication that cannot share the effect class, effect pressure, allocation cancellation or structural fault |
| Compute exchange | canonical completion order followed by role-compatible checkout over the virtual post-completion state | completed owners and newly checked-out owners in one total delta; the same owner may compose `Computing -> Queued(Verify) -> Computing` | any member whose policy reaches outside its owner cohort is isolated and processed by the existing exact planner |
| Final membership | exact validated receipts plus chain-backed, pairwise-disjoint Cell read/write proof | longest contiguous strongest-first prefix whose members commute with current Accepted membership and each other | RBF, pool parent/child, conditional reader/spender edge, proposal collision, payload refresh requiring distinct retirement, or capacity eviction |
| Effects | one committed effect class and startup-proved indivisible bound | exact effects produced by the same owner batch | different capacity/trust class or a batch that cannot fit its construction bound |
| Query/read projection | immutable authority cut or exact bounded receipt | independent readers and projections over one coherent source cut | only a requested consistency boundary; no query may acquire mutation authority or globally invalidate disjoint work |
| Template publication | exact pool/component source receipt plus template OCC | uncle/proposal/transaction partial lanes remain optimistic and concurrent | full/reset share their required serial replacement lock; stale partial work is discarded rather than serializing all lanes |
| Chain/reorg | ordered chain command and one complete reconciliation cohort | preparation, validation and unrelated external work remain lock-external | ownership, dependency, proposal-window and recovery effects commit as one chain Apply; no partial chain publication |

The proof types are ephemeral Plan inputs. They are never stored in
`OwnedTx`, persisted, exposed by RPC or used as a second scheduler.

## 7. Current lifecycle and serialization audit

The common retained path currently performs these authoritative operations:

| Boundary | Current operation | Semantic necessity | Batch opportunity |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Remote/Proposal admission | one write Plan/Apply per owner | ownership and continuous charge must precede hostile work | upstream already supplies bounded batches; simulate canonical outcomes and Apply once |
| Queued checkout | one write Plan/Apply per worker job | exact lease and active-work charge are required | assign all currently idle compatible slots from one scheduler/resource cut |
| Resolve-to-Verify | zero Apply only when a verifier owns `ResolveThenVerify`; otherwise settlement plus later checkout | verification needs resolved facts; no membership decision occurs | compose settlement and compatible re-checkout in the same exchange |
| Computing completion | one write Plan/Apply per owner | capability must settle exactly once | drain available completions and commit the batchable set together |
| Ready validation capture | two shared reads for a bounded slice | overlay and snapshot must be coherent; allocation must stay outside mutation | retain OCC capture; increase the useful prefix, not the number of cuts |
| Ready membership | one write; at most eight owners, but one coupled tail collapses the whole run to the head | RBF/capacity/dependency coupling must be atomic | commit the longest contiguous proven-commuting prefix up to the common authority batch bound |
| Effect publication | claim-bound read, external I/O, one settlement write per committed effect batch | I/O cannot be in Apply and progress must be exact | already amortized by the membership batch; retain |

This gives an ordinary one-owner Remote transaction approximately five
Applies: admission, checkout, completion to Ready, membership, and effect
settlement. Only membership is currently amortized, and often not in practice
because completion wakes the Ready driver one owner at a time.

The current delta families are not one accidental hierarchy:

- `EntryDelta` is a one-owner phase transition;
- `IndependentDelta` is a commuting Ready-to-Accepted set;
- `MembershipDelta` is an exact RBF/capacity cohort;
- `OwnerRemovalBatch`/`AdminDelta` is a cause-complete removal cohort;
- `ChainDelta` is a chain-generation reconciliation.

They have different proof obligations and must not be collapsed into an
untyped mega-delta. The missing abstraction is narrower: a bounded ordered set
of homogeneous `PreAccepted` phase transitions with optional worker handoff.
It may reuse the existing batch projection planners without making RBF,
administration or chain policy generic.

The final target for a saturated chain-backed workload is:

```text
bounded ingress       one Apply per upstream microbatch
initial checkout      one Apply per available worker wave
steady compute        one exchange Apply settles the completed wave and
                      checks out the next wave
final membership      one commuting Apply per Ready prefix
effect settlement     one Apply per committed effect batch
```

No claim is made that arbitrary single arrivals or staggered completions must
wait to form a full batch. The hard contract is that already available work is
not forced through one complete authority round trip per owner.

