## 9. Non-normative candidate notebook: bounded semantic exchange

This section preserves the M8 design exploration and falsifiers. It is not an
implementation authorization or a claim that its coordinator, channels,
`Ready` representation or task topology is necessary. D1/D2 may fuse, replace
or reject any part. Only the D3 winner can become normative.

### 9.1 Stable state

The persistent owner algebra is unchanged:

```text
Nowhere | PreAccepted(Queued | Computing | Waiting | Ready)
        | Accepted(Pending | Gap | Proposed)
        | ReplacementHistory
```

No `Independent`, `Completed`, `Batch`, `WorkerOwned` or DAG-resident owner
variant is added. Resource charge still follows owner existence, and active
compute charge still follows the `Computing` phase.

### 9.2 Runtime topology

One `ComputeCoordinator` becomes the sole retained-compute checkout and
settlement caller. It owns no transaction map and no policy. It owns only:

- a bounded set of stable worker-slot identifiers and roles;
- one bounded completion channel whose capacity is the number of workers;
- one capacity-one assignment channel per worker;
- at most one move-only completion or assignment capability per worker;
- deterministic transient scratch bounded by the common authority batch
  limit.

Workers execute resolve and verification only. They never acquire the
authority write lock and never decide settlement. Pool-produced dependency
enrichment may still consume the existing bounded coherent read receipt; this
does not create mutation authority. A worker returns its exact completion,
execution permit and optional cache update to the coordinator.
The coordinator drains all completions already available at the first wake,
forms one exchange, and immediately returns assignments. There is no batch
timer, sleep or minimum fill level.

The ordinary owner batch ceiling is the existing
`MAX_POOL_MUTATION_CANDIDATES` value (100), renamed to express that shared
meaning. A compute exchange handles at most `min(active_work_slots, 100)`
owners and immediately loops when more slots are configured. Retained ingress
uses at most 100 owners. Ready membership uses at most
`min(active_work_slots, 100)` owners: a larger capture cannot be produced by
one completion wave and increases proof work without increasing compute
occupancy. The hard ceiling still matches the existing 101-effect startup
bound for one candidate plus its bounded mutation cohort. These are
resource/proof limits, not timing knobs.

The existing count-only semaphore remains because synchronous Local work and
retained work share the configured transient compute envelope. Idle worker
slots hold no semaphore permit. A completion retains its permit until the
coordinator classifies its exact settlement. The permit must never transfer
directly from one completed execution to a later execution: that bypasses an
older synchronous Local waiter in Tokio's fair semaphore and can starve Local
under a continuous retained wave. After classification, every completion
consumes its execution capability into a finished-work capability, returns the
count token to the fair semaphore, and may use only a token that the
coordinator subsequently reacquires. If a queued Local caller wins that token,
the exchange settles the completion without a replacement checkout. The
derived law `executing + finished <= active_work_slots` prevents publisher
backpressure from accumulating more completed results or checking out new
retained work into an occupied slot. Therefore an effect-capacity-blocked
completion retains only its original bounded worker slot, not the semaphore
permit; retaining both would transfer effect pressure into Direct starvation.

When queued work exists, the coordinator awaits at most one fair semaphore
acquisition and then uses non-blocking acquisitions for the rest of the
currently idle bounded slice. It never queues one semaphore waiter per idle
worker. Every completed count token passes through the same fair semaphore
before it can guard another execution, so an older Local waiter receives the
released token before coordinator `try_acquire` calls can observe it. If the
exact exchange finds no compatible owner, every unused permit is released
immediately.

Role matching remains deterministic:

1. preserve the dedicated ordered Resolve capability;
2. assign Small verification to a Small-capable slot before consuming an Any
   slot when both choices preserve the same scheduler order;
3. assign Large verification only to Any;
4. let remaining verifier slots take `ResolveThenVerify` work;
5. advance each fair-lane cursor exactly as the canonical sequential checkout
   would.

The existing verification pause/resume command is an assignment gate owned by
the coordinator and an execution input owned by running verifiers. Pause does
not discard a capability or close a channel; resume rechecks the authoritative
level before dispatch.

### 9.3 Compute exchange

`ComputeExchangePlan` consumes two bounded sets: completed capabilities and
available worker roles. Its canonical order is:

1. validate and classify completions by exact owner/version/phase/view;
2. give a fitting cohort-wide peer revocation exclusive precedence for one
   bounded Apply, because it may invalidate other completions from that peer;
   if its Critical region is full, retain that exact capability and exclude
   same-peer work without blocking unrelated no-effect progress; partition
   remaining owner-local publications by effect class and, among classes that
   currently fit, choose the class containing the earliest retained `Arrival`
   rather than a static trust-class priority, then include compatible
   same-class effects;
3. construct the virtual post-completion scheduler and resource view;
4. select compatible queued work for available permits in fair order;
5. compose a same-owner Resolve-to-Verify handoff when the virtual result and
   worker role permit it;
6. compile unique final owner updates, exact resource/scheduler/dependency/
   index/source deltas, one Apply sequence and distinct entry versions;
7. Apply once, release large retired values after the guard opens, publish one
   derived wake transition, then deliver assignments/cache updates.

The exchange is bounded by configured active-work slots and the common
authority mutation ceiling. It never scans the full owner population. A
completion that cannot join the common batch retains its move-only capability
in one worker-bounded pending slot and is routed through the existing exact
single/cohort planner. It cannot make unrelated batchable completions wait.

Assignment delivery is capability transport, not part of Apply. If a
capacity-one worker channel closes after checkout commits, failed `send`
returns the exact move-only assignment capability to the coordinator. The
coordinator immediately compiles the ordinary cancellation/requeue transition;
it does not roll back the committed Apply, lose the owner, or invalidate the
service. A running worker likewise returns its completion/capability when
cancelled; an idle worker owns no transaction capability and exits directly.

### 9.4 Retained ingress batch

The relayer's already validated transaction message and `NotifyTxBatch` are
split into non-empty authority microbatches no larger than the common mutation
ceiling. Non-contextual checks run outside the guard. Inside one Plan, items
are simulated in canonical message order, including duplicates, Proposal
promotion, proposal-ID collision, resource pressure and effects. One total
Apply commits the resulting owner/effect prefix.

Remote batches are homogeneous by peer. If any item is malformed, one peer
revocation transition wins: no owner from that not-yet-applied batch survives,
all affected known-filter entries are released, and the same transaction can
later arrive from another peer. Accepted owners are never removed by this
rule. This is the existing ban contract expressed at the batch boundary.

The ordered Plan must compile the same per-item public outcomes as canonical
message-order submission. In particular, a later malformed item observes and
revokes earlier same-batch PreAccepted owners, remaining items observe the
virtual ban, and reject/release effects are not silently collapsed. If one
effect class cannot hold the whole canonical outcome, the Plan commits only
the longest complete prefix or returns a typed no-mutation capacity outcome;
it never partially publishes an item.

Both retained network paths currently mutate relay state before controller
enqueue. Remote ingress marks transaction hashes known; Proposal ingress also
removes the matching in-flight proposal rows. The batch handoff must therefore
own an exact inverse or delay those mutations until acknowledged authority
retention. Enqueue failure returns the exact batch capability to the caller;
Proposal recovery must also retain the original in-flight evidence rather than
reconstructing it from a boolean. A legal no-owner authority disposition must
explicitly settle the upstream projection as well. Neither controller pressure
nor authority resource pressure may leave a pre-authority hash pinned until
TTL or consume the only proposal-window request without a durable owner.

The public one-transaction controller method remains a one-element batch, so
Local callers and single-item latency do not wait for aggregation.

### 9.5 Ready membership batch

Ready remains necessary: it releases scarce compute capacity before final
membership validation and lets the Ready driver overlap with later compute.
The direct-finalization experiment showed that deleting this boundary holds a
compute slot across membership and regresses throughput; that experiment does
not argue against batched completion.

The driver captures up to `min(active_work_slots, 100)` owners in strict Ready
order. It commits the longest contiguous prefix that has:

- exact current owner/version/view receipts;
- shared payload retirement semantics;
- chain-backed inputs and dependencies;
- no Accepted parent, child, spender or conditional reader relation;
- no pairwise shared input, causal edge or conditional read/write edge;
- no capacity eviction and one fitting effect class.

The first member that can change the outcome of a later member ends the run.
This preserves canonical strict-priority semantics while preventing an
unrelated coupled tail near the end of a captured slice from collapsing the
whole prefix to one. The exact coupled planner handles the head when the first
member itself is not independent.

The classifier is one incremental bounded scan, not an O(B squared) pairwise
matrix. It inserts each accepted prefix member's candidate hash and exact
`W/R` keys into ephemeral scratch sets, rejects the first intersection or
conditional relation, and performs the final exact Accepted-index checks.
Scratch is prepared outside the authority guard where possible and discarded
after Plan; it is not a resident DAG or decision cache.

### 9.6 Liveness, cancellation and shutdown

The wait-for graph is closed:

| Waiter | Level waited for | Unique releaser |
| --- | --- | --- |
| compute coordinator with no eligible work | authoritative queued level | admission, dependency, chain or completion Apply |
| compute coordinator with work but no execution permit | semaphore capacity | Local completion or committed retained completion/cancellation |
| effect-blocked completion | exact effect region capacity | sole effect publisher settlement |
| worker | assignment or cancellation | coordinator or topology cancellation |
| generation supervisor | coordinator task exit or join failure | cancel the generation, recover or retire every returned linear capability, and forbid persistence if closure cannot be proved |
| Ready driver | Ready level/effect capacity | compute exchange or publisher settlement |

Every wait subscribes before its authoritative level check. The coordinator
processes a first event and drains only what is already available; it never
waits for a batch peer. Stale completion, chain change, clear, peer revocation
and local removal are ordinary capability discharge outcomes.

Shutdown order is fixed: stop ingress; stop new checkout; ask workers to
return their current capabilities; settle, cancel or prove stale every
capability; join workers; close and drain effects; then capture persistence.
The coordinator channel cannot close while a live Computing capability lacks
a receiver. If it closes unexpectedly, `SendError` returns the capability to
the supervised owner and the same cancellation settlement runs. A legal
transaction or supported cancellation cannot terminate the service.

The coordinator is a single compute-feed task, but it does not newly widen the
current operational failure domain: production already maps an unexpected
exit or join failure of any authority worker to generation invalidation rather
than continuing at `W - 1` capacity. The candidate must refine that same
generation-level shutdown and capability-closure rule. It concentrates the
immediate assignment pause in one task, so M3.6 must still compare recovery
latency and closure work; it may not claim partial worker degradation as the
current baseline.

