### 9.9 Comparator topology: self-fused workers

The coordinator-free hedge keeps the current worker set. After finishing one
execution, a worker converts the execution capability to a finished-work
capability, returns the count token to the shared fair semaphore, and may only
use a token acquired again through that semaphore. Holding both the finished
capability and the newly acquired token authorizes one atomic Apply that
settles the old owner and, when compatible work exists, checks out the next
owner for that worker. If the worker does not reacquire, or if effect capacity
blocks settlement, it settles without replacement checkout and holds no
semaphore token. This is observationally one legal adjacent
`settle; checkout` serialization and preserves the scheduler cursor and older
Local waiter order.

The hedge removes one authority round trip per steady worker completion but
does not combine different workers' completions. Its dominant authority work
therefore remains Theta(transactions), whereas the semantic exchange targets
Theta(ceiling(transactions / bounded_wave)) for already available independent
work. M3.6 retains it because it has no new task, channel or failure domain and
can falsify whether cross-worker exchange complexity earns its cost. It is not
allowed to satisfy T16's wave-level operation-count gate by relabeling the
per-worker Apply.

