## 11. Safety audit that precedes implementation

The current broad `AuthorityFault` and generation-stop surfaces are not
accepted on trust. Before the exchange compiler lands, generate every
production constructor and trace backward to its external inputs. Each site
must end in exactly one class:

- construction-impossible programmer defect, with the type/owner proof and a
  negative model test;
- ordinary stale work, policy rejection, allocation/resource pressure or
  external failure, returned through a typed non-fatal boundary;
- true integrity loss whose only supported response is generation invalidation.

Any legal or hostile input reaching the third class blocks implementation and
must be removed at its producer boundary. In particular, broad conversions
from `PlanError` may not turn an unreviewed new variant into fail-stop. Public
planner methods expose operation-specific closed error enums at the producer;
downstream `from_plan` adapters may not classify an open `PlanError` family.
Projection modules remain the only normal constructors of projection faults.
Static AST checks discover constructor/boundary facts and fail on a new
constructor or conversion boundary, while semantic classification lives beside
the owning type rather than in a copied line-number allowlist.

The historical V2 pipeline is execution-topology evidence only. Its reported
single-peer secp gain came from parallel precheck/verification and handoff, but
the measurement is not a controlled current-versus-`develop` release result,
and its duplicate queues plus panic-catching failure protocol are rejected.
Only the topology lesson may influence D2; none of its safety mechanisms or
performance numbers is an acceptance premise.

Every D1 adjudication is an evidence record, not prose consensus. Its required
fields are:

| Field | Authoritative evidence |
| --- | --- |
| Semantic rule | raw/resolved/verified CKB type and protocol behavior that owns the rule |
| Production producer | AST-discovered constructor and enclosing function/module; no copied line allowlist |
| External reachability | complete caller path from controller, worker, chain, publisher, persistence or admin input |
| Impossibility or ordinary-outcome proof | sealed type/validated constructor/exact precondition, or the closed non-fatal error variant returned at the producer |
| State and resource consequence | owner, charge, projection, effect and capability before/after |
| Falsifier | negative model/unit test that would reach the bad outcome if the premise were removed |
| End-to-end anchor | production-route nextest or managed integration scenario when the boundary is externally reachable |
| Drift gate | generated selector/check that fails on a new constructor, open conversion, zero-match test or unowned rule |

An item is `PROVEN` only when all applicable fields point to exact source and
passing evidence. `SUPPORTED` means the code argument is plausible but a
falsifier or drift gate is still absent; it blocks D1 exit. `OPEN` means the
reachability or disposition is unresolved; it blocks implementation. A test
alone cannot prove constructor impossibility, and a type argument alone cannot
prove the public route preserves the intended outcome.

Task topology is part of the same table. A worker, ordered-chain or publisher
exit is not accepted as safe merely because the join layer types it: the proof
must name who owns every sender/receiver and cancellation capability, why legal
input cannot close it, and how every move-only transaction/effect capability is
returned or settled first. Derived template/cache failure remains isolated only
if it owns no lifecycle or publication capability.

## 12. Adjudication discipline

Section 8 is the frozen comparison, not permission to implement the selected
mechanism without trying to falsify it. D2 repeats the comparison after the
semantic and fault audits because either can expose a missing coupling edge.
Models may be fused only when the combined proof is smaller than the separate
protocols. For every retained or rejected model record:

- primary owner and every transient capability;
- exact state/transition algebra;
- locks, atomics, tasks, queues, channels and cancellation owner;
- batch formation and maximum size;
- single-item latency and head-of-line behavior;
- resource charging and release time;
- stale member, partial completion and worker death behavior;
- RBF/dependency/chain/effect fallback;
- asymptotic and per-transaction authority operation counts;
- new failure domains and whether risk is eliminated, bounded or transferred;
- source/test/document delta and deletion opportunity.

The selected model remains selected only while it is the smallest model
satisfying both hard gates. A contradiction reopens section 8; it does not
produce a compensating flag or exception inside section 9.

