#### M3.2 bounded footprint refinement domain

M3.2 uses a finite cell-role algebra rather than a collection of named
examples. For one cell and one owner, the only legal semantic roles at the
Ready cut are `None`, `Input`, `Read` and `Output`. `Input` and `Read` are
disjoint because production removes inputs from the expanded dependency set;
an `Output` is derived from the raw transaction identity and output index.
Header dependencies are a separate read-only role. The differential domain
enumerates the complete 4 x 4 relation table for candidate/candidate and
candidate/Accepted pairs, the chain/pool evidence axis, shared headers,
source/effect-control class, current/stale evidence, Accepted capacity and
effect capacity. It then enumerates every single coupled-edge position in the
maximum Ready bound and every coupled-edge subset through four owners. The
classifier is a monotone disjunction of those per-cell relations, so larger
multi-edge cohorts add no new truth-table row; the test must mechanically
check that monotonicity rather than assume it in prose.

For ordered footprints `f[0..n]`, let `A(f)` mean a relation to current
Accepted membership, `P(f_i, f_j)` a pairwise coupling relation, `R(k)` the
aggregate Accepted-resource exclusion at prefix `k`, and `E(k)` the exact
next effect-batch exclusion. The required prefix is:

```text
k_star = max { k <= n |
  for every i < k: not A(f_i)
  and for every i < j < k: not P(f_i, f_j)
  and not R(k)
  and not E(k)
  and all f[0..k] share the selected effect-control class and current evidence
}
```

Both adapters independently construct their own state and call the real model
and production planners. They may share only the generated finite input
domain and normalized public observation. Extracting the classifier into one
shared implementation would turn the differential proof into a tautology and
is forbidden.

Two identity cases are outside the shared legal domain and remain explicit
premises rather than forged production fixtures. Different raw identities
cannot produce the same exact outpoint, because output identity is
`(RawTxHash, output_index)`. Distinct resident owners cannot share one proposal
short ID, because the proposal index rejects the second owner before Ready.
The executable model must classify such hostile abstract states as invalid or
unrepresentable; production must bind the exclusion to the sealed identity
constructor and proposal-index transition. A raw-hash match with an invalid
output index is likewise excluded by the resolver's positive cell-evidence
contract, not by a classifier convention.

