##### M3.3 startup trace-cut correction

The first trace call-graph audit found a reference-model omission that must be
closed before any replay claim. Production `ResolveThenVerify` keeps one
move-only checkout token through resolution and VM verification. The
resolve-to-verify handoff performs no authority Apply; the resident owner stays
`Computing(ActiveWork { permit: ResolveThenVerify, .. })`, while the linear
capability alone gains the resolved evidence. The existing model expresses
only split Resolve settlement followed by Verify checkout, consuming two extra
Apply stamps. Because Apply stamps, owner phase and linear capabilities are in
`Obs`, a trace quotient may not erase that difference.

The smallest correction is typed rather than a special-case counter rule:

```text
Computing owner = (transaction, version, work_permit)
live capability = (transaction, version, work_permit, current_stage, evidence)
resolved evidence = (..., verify_cycle_class)
permits(Any, Small | Large) = true
permits(SmallCycleOnly, Small) = true
permits(SmallCycleOnly, Large) = false

ResolveOnly permits only Resolve on the split route
VerifyOnly(cap) permits Verify(evidence) iff permits(cap, evidence.verify_cycle_class)
ResolveThenVerify(cap) permits Resolve -> Verify(evidence) without Apply
  iff permits(cap, evidence.verify_cycle_class)
ResolveThenVerify(cap) otherwise settles to queued Verify with one Apply

ContinueResolveThenVerify:
  authority' = authority
  capability.stage' = Verify(evidence)
  apply_stamp' = apply_stamp
```

Checkout constructs only a permit/stage-compatible capability. Split Resolve
still settles to queued Verify with one Apply. Compatible continuous Resolve
changes only the unique linear capability and final Verify settles directly to
Ready with one Apply. A `SmallCycleOnly` token that resolves `Large` work
cannot take that transition and instead consumes its token into queued Verify
with one Apply, exactly like production. Cancellation, stale evidence and
failure consume that same capability exactly once. This correction adds no
production state and does not select a later topology; it makes the reference
kernel capable of stating every existing legal production route before trace
refinement begins.

This correction does not silently promote the provisional compute-exchange
candidate. Its current `RetainedPermitGrant` is a count-only fair-scheduler
token and `plan_compute_exchange` requests only the split checkout route, so it
cannot yet prove role-compatible `ResolveThenVerify` assignment. M3.3 must
either add the worker-role/verification-capability fact to that ephemeral
grant and refine the exact production scheduler, or narrow the exchange
theorem to split work and leave continuous assignment as an M3.6 candidate
