##### M3.3 step 3 scheduler/permit quotient design freeze

The scheduler proof keeps three linear resources distinct. A fair compute
permit owns one slot in the Direct/retained execution envelope; an idle worker
slot owns one stable execution role; an authority work capability owns one
`Computing` transaction and its settlement right. Pairing them authorizes an
assignment but never merges their identities or conservation laws:

```text
free_permits + active_permits = configured_compute_capacity
idle_worker_slots + assigned_worker_slots + finished_worker_slots
    = configured_retained_worker_slots
queued_owners + computing_owners + waiting_owners + ready_owners
    = retained_owners
one assignment = one fair permit + one idle compatible worker slot
               + one selected queued owner -> one work capability
```

The existing `RetainedPermitGrant` remains only the result of fair count
acquisition. It cannot enter `ComputeExchangePlan` directly. A sealed
test-model binding consumes that count batch plus a unique ephemeral worker
slot set and produces role-bearing grants. Rejection returns every count token
and slot unchanged. Worker role is never stored in an owner, resource row,
RPC view or persistent scheduler projection.

The independent scheduler quotient derives its runnable entries from one
`Omega` cut and stores only the two temporal owner cursors already present in
production: Resolve and Verify. Its role order is fixed: the single ordered
resolver selects `ResolveOnly`; a small-only verifier selects compatible
Verify before fallback `ResolveThenVerify(SmallCycleOnly)`; an Any verifier
selects compatible Verify before fallback `ResolveThenVerify(Any)`. Small
verification is offered to a small-only slot before an Any slot. Every
selected owner is removed from the ephemeral wave view before the next slot is
considered. The model reproduces the production Trusted-first initial cut,
per-lane owner ring, configured Verify order and deterministic identity ties.

The quotient emits a sealed exact-owner checkout intent. The reference kernel
validates and consumes that intent; it does not rescan a flat queue or copy the
scheduler transition. `ComputeExchangePlan` first folds completions, derives
the role-compatible wave from the virtual post-completion cut, then proves the
combined canonical `settle; assigned checkout` sequence. Apply validates both
the `Omega` cut and scheduler-cursor cut before mutating either. Staleness
returns every role-bearing grant and changes neither authority.

Production replay uses the real `FairFrontier` through deterministic legal
sequential checkout calls. It compares symbolic transaction, worker slot,
permit, lane and resulting cursor after every selection. Fixtures cover
Trusted plus multiple Remote owners, small plus large Verify work, verifier
fallback to continuous Resolve, cursor wrap and an input permutation of idle
slots. This proves equality with one named no-interleaving production
serialization; it does not claim current worker lock acquisition order is
deterministic.

The external fair-permit model is generalized from one owner to a configured
multi-owner capacity. It must preserve FIFO Direct precedence when retained
work finishes, allow unrelated retained workers to remain active, bound
finished slots independently of released permits and retain the required
effect-full/Direct-handoff negative witness. Rust tests cover mixed domains,
duplicate permit identity, duplicate worker slots, role/count mismatch,
small-worker/large-work rejection, stale Apply and exact token return.

The step also closes a process-model drift found by the call-graph audit. The
roadmap requires every TLA module/configuration and expected verdict to be
discovered, but `check_formal_models.py` still names `PermitEffect.tla` and two
configs directly. A machine-readable manifest becomes the sole verdict owner;
the checker discovers every `.tla` and `.cfg`, rejects unregistered or missing
files and validates one exact success or named negative witness per config.

This step is model, test and evidence work only. It adds no production state,
lock, task, queue, branch or hot-path cost. Failure to obtain exact scheduler
equality reopens the M8 exchange candidate rather than adding normalization or
persistent worker metadata.

