## 14. Strengthened execution discipline

The following gates correct the process failures in section 2:

1. **Two-axis status.** Every checkpoint reports `safety` and `objective`
   separately. Neither can imply the other.
2. **Normative MUST graph.** Every top-level goal has a stable ID, exact code
   owner, producer path, falsification test and integration scenario. CI rejects
   a missing or zero-match edge.
3. **No permissive hard requirements.** `may`, `can` and `optional` cannot
   express an acceptance goal. Optional features must name the decision owner.
4. **End-to-end proof.** A helper/model test is insufficient. At least one test
   must exercise the production route and prove the promised transition count
   and concurrent worker occupancy.
5. **Negative feature test.** At each review ask whether deleting or bypassing
   the claimed feature would still pass all gates. If yes, the gate is invalid.
6. **Semantic restart.** Whole-architecture review begins from transaction
   read/write facts and public behavior, then maps into code. It may not begin
   only from current type names.
7. **Static performance gate before benchmark.** Stage transitions, authority
   acquisitions, scans, allocations and wake topology are audited and counted
   before timing. Benchmark validates a complete mechanism; it does not reveal
   that the mechanism is absent.
8. **Candidate comparison before code.** At least the status quo and two
   materially different complete models are falsified before production
   implementation.
9. **Design-code ordering.** A new architecture type cannot land before its
   normative rule and acceptance evidence are frozen. CI must make the order
   observable.
10. **No current-model circular proof.** Internal consistency is necessary but
    never sufficient; every phase must still answer the original business and
    attack goal.
11. **Deletion ledger.** Every new mechanism names what old ceremony it
    replaces. Net growth is accepted only with a proven new obligation.
12. **Contradiction response.** A failed architectural premise reopens design;
    it is not patched forward.
13. **Progress durability.** This plan, `.release-progress`, Git checkpoint,
    completed evidence and next exact action are updated together at every
    phase boundary.
14. **Whole-model phase gate.** M0-M4, every I-slice and every V/P phase ends
    with the section 3.8 model-delta audit, then re-derives the design from CKB
    transaction semantics and checks the frozen end-to-end objective, single
    authority, composition theorem, coupled fallback, legal-input failure
    domain, historical attack closure, concurrency/lock cost, operation count,
    compatibility, evidence graph and deletion ledger. The review must
    distinguish a model gap, production refinement bug and trusted-boundary
    failure. Any drift reopens the owning M phase before code continues; it
    never creates a compensating flag, retry or repair structure.
15. **Superseded decisions are explicit.** Every durable decision has a stable
    identifier. Replacing it records `SUPERSEDED BY <identifier>` and leaves
    exactly one current owner; restart recovery never infers the latest rule
    from narrative order. The normative plan is current authority and
    `.release-progress` is append-only evidence, not a competing decision
    source.
16. **Model changes are reviewed at the change checkpoint.** Any semantic
    change under `src/tests/model/` or `formal/` records the affected law,
    premise and observation (or proves a mechanical no-semantic-change move),
    reruns its negative falsifier and the section 3.8 questions before the
    checkpoint. A commit-message token is useful review metadata but is not a
    correctness gate; path-triggered generated bindings, tests and model-delta
    evidence are the gate.
17. **Release surfaces are evidence graph nodes.** Public RPC documentation,
    per-crate changelogs, default configuration templates, supported migration
    notes and invoked deployment smoke commands are derived review targets,
    not manual afterthoughts. CI rejects a changed compatibility/configuration/
    persistence term that has no owning release surface; it must not infer a
    semantic claim merely from a changelog sentence.

## 15. Mandatory acceptance observations

The exact constants are selected in D3, but the final evidence must prove:

- a bounded independent set produces authority writes proportional to batch
  count, with a documented upper bound independent of transaction count inside
  each batch;
- all configured workers can execute independent verification concurrently;
- the preceding occupancy claim applies when no committed-effect region is
  saturated and shutdown/cancellation is inactive. Under effect backpressure,
  at most one exact pending completion and compute permit per worker may be
  retained; concurrency may fall to publisher drain rate, but unrelated
  no-effect completions must still make bounded progress;
- no batch waits for a slow member before already completed members can make
  bounded progress;
- a one-item workload requires no timer delay;
- one stale/conflicting/resource-blocked member cannot invalidate or strand
  unrelated valid members; it is excluded or routed to coupled fallback before
  Apply;
- RBF victims and candidate, dependency cohorts, chain updates and effect
  capacity retain atomic outcomes;
- peer-controlled memory and work remain charged and bounded while capabilities
  are outside the guard;
- worker cancellation/death returns or stales every capability without a
  second owner or service fail-stop;
- operation-count tests fail if the implementation silently regresses to a
  full per-owner transition sequence;
- fixed-binary scaling and latency evidence confirm the static model.
- chain-revision tests vary active worker count and completion state to measure
  bounded stale work. Batch-ceiling experiments separately measure Apply hold
  and p99 chain-update/read latency. The batch ceiling is not presumed to
  increase reorg rework: without a fill timer, in-flight work is bounded by
  active execution slots regardless of how many ready completions one Apply
  can drain;
- a workload may be called VM-bound only when identical semantic VM work,
  hardware saturation and negligible control-plane idle time are demonstrated.
  Parity with `develop` is optimal only under that evidence; otherwise parity
  does not close the performance objective.

